1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:11,200
A large language model is like the autocomplete on your phone, trained on a huge library of text. It predicts the next word, again and again.

2
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:20,975
It does not look facts up. It predicts what sounds right. That is why it can be confidently wrong, which we call hallucinating.

3
00:00:22,670 --> 00:00:31,670
Temperature controls how adventurous the model is. Low temperature picks the most likely words: good for facts and data.

4
00:00:32,370 --> 00:00:38,445
Higher temperature takes more chances: good for brainstorming and creative writing.

5
00:00:39,150 --> 00:00:41,850
For business tasks, start low.

6
00:00:43,550 --> 00:00:51,750
Models read text in small pieces called tokens, roughly three quarters of a word each. You pay per token.

7
00:00:52,450 --> 00:01:04,325
Everything the model knows about your conversation must fit in its context window, like a desk with limited space. Clear instructions in, better answers out.

